 # Gradient-Based Surface Shading
 
-Also called: Gradient-Based Shading (Levoy), Grey-level gradient shading, Gray-level Shading
+Also called: Gradient-Based Shading (Levoy), Gray-Scale Gradient Shading, Gray-level Shading, Grey-level gradient shading, Hohne shading, Object space shading, Phong Shading + Depth Cueing
 
 ## Overview
-Gradient-Based Surface Shading is a local illumination technique for volume rendering that approximates surface normals using the gradient of the scalar field to apply Phong-style lighting (diffuse, specular, and ambient) [S3][S5, unofficial]. Unlike standard emission/absorption models which treat voxels as light sources or absorbers, this method makes surface details and boundaries more prominent by calculating lighting based on local orientation [S3, S11]. It sets itself apart from its parent method, [Gradient Estimation from Samples](2180-gradient-estimation-from-samples.md), by specifically utilizing these estimated gradients to drive a shading model that mimics polygonal graphics, and from its sibling [Gradient-based Pre-computed Shading](3159-gradient-based-pre-computed-shading.md) by typically computing gradients on-the-fly or trading memory for time via precomputation [S4, unofficial].
+Gradient-Based Surface Shading is a local illumination technique for volume rendering that approximates surface normals using the gradient of the scalar field to apply Phong-style lighting (diffuse, specular, and ambient) [S3, unofficial][S5, unofficial]. Unlike standard emission/absorption models which treat voxels as light sources or absorbers, this method makes surface details and boundaries more prominent by calculating lighting based on local orientation [S4]. It sets itself apart from its parent method, [Gradient Estimation from Samples](2180-gradient-estimation-from-samples.md), by specifically utilizing these estimated gradients to drive a shading model that mimics polygonal graphics, and from its sibling [Gradient-based Pre-computed Shading](3159-gradient-based-pre-computed-shading.md) by typically computing gradients on-the-fly or trading memory for time via precomputation [S4].
 
